WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.130
The strongest destination-validation
design is often to remove the destination from

2
00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:06.098
client control.

3
00:00:06.178 --> 00:00:10.363
Instead of exposing a generic fetch
endpoint, expose the business operation.

4
00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:14.249
A route that sends through a named
integration, tests a stored webhook,

5
00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:18.768
or retrieves an object from a supported
provider lets the server select the network

6
00:00:18.848 --> 00:00:21.131
destination from trusted configuration.

7
00:00:21.211 --> 00:00:23.241
The client chooses an allowed action;

8
00:00:23.321 --> 00:00:26.048
it does not receive a socket-by-proxy
capability.

9
00:00:26.128 --> 00:00:29.870
Server-managed integration records make
this separation concrete.

10
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.248
A record can hold an approved base URL,
credential reference,

11
00:00:33.328 --> 00:00:36.435
permitted method, tenant owner, and
operational limits.

12
00:00:36.515 --> 00:00:40.511
The request supplies an opaque integration
identifier and business data.

13
00:00:40.591 --> 00:00:42.430
The application loads the record,

14
00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.872
verifies that the current principal may
use it for this action,

15
00:00:45.952 --> 00:00:48.552
and constructs the request from trusted
fields.

16
00:00:48.632 --> 00:00:50.852
The identifier is not self-authorizing.

17
00:00:50.932 --> 00:00:55.371
Guess-resistant values still require
tenant, user, state, and operation checks.

18
00:00:55.451 --> 00:00:57.671
Provider selection can be narrow as well.

19
00:00:57.751 --> 00:01:02.254
A client may choose a logical provider
name or remote object identifier while server

20
00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:04.680
code maps that choice to a fixed API base
URL.

21
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.073
Paths and query values can be constructed
according to that provider's contract

22
00:01:09.153 --> 00:01:11.563
without allowing them to replace the
origin.

23
00:01:11.643 --> 00:01:15.956
This approach turns a nearly unlimited
network request into a small application

24
00:01:16.036 --> 00:01:19.334
capability that is easier to reason about,
test, and monitor.

25
00:01:19.414 --> 00:01:23.282
Some products genuinely need a public-URL
importer or preview service.

26
00:01:23.362 --> 00:01:24.948
Treat that as an exceptional,

27
00:01:25.028 --> 00:01:28.960
higher-risk capability and define its
envelope before implementation:

28
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.226
protocols, ports, destination classes,
methods, redirect count, timeouts,

29
00:01:33.306 --> 00:01:37.935
response and decompression limits, content
expectations, and credential behavior.

30
00:01:38.015 --> 00:01:42.138
Even an approved destination may not be
appropriate for every caller or every

31
00:01:42.218 --> 00:01:42.852
operation.

32
00:01:42.932 --> 00:01:46.547
Destination policy and application
authorization must both pass.

33
00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:50.749
Avoiding arbitrary URLs reduces the number
of parser, DNS, redirect, proxy,

34
00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:53.620
and network states that security code must
control.
